1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Lesson 28: survey and the boom correction in reverse

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:31,427
YA-NEC course, lesson 28: the survey of an antenna that is already built, and the boom correction applied in reverse.

3
00:00:31,477 --> 00:00:43,494
The survey, on the real antenna: the length of every element tip to tip, its position along the boom starting from the reflector, the element diameter with a caliper.

4
00:00:43,544 --> 00:00:57,827
Then the boom: round or square, and its size. And how the elements are mounted: through the boom in contact, through it insulated, or above it. For the radiator: whether it is insulated from the boom.

5
00:00:57,877 --> 00:01:11,561
Example: the 10-element bvo by IC8TEM built on a 30 mm round boom, 4 mm elements through the boom in contact, 8 mm radiator insulated. These are the measurements you would read with the tape.

6
00:01:11,611 --> 00:01:38,506
New, and in the Wires table one row per element: X is the position on the boom, Y1 and Y2 half the length, then the radius in mm, that is half the measured diameter, and the segments.

7
00:01:38,556 --> 00:02:16,944
Ten rows like these, one per element. Apply and recalculate, then the source at the centre of the radiator: w2c.

8
00:02:16,994 --> 00:02:29,041
Calculated as it is, the survey gives 11.9 − j44.6 Ω, SWR 7.6 and F/B 10.5 dB: this is not the antenna that works on the roof.

9
00:02:29,091 --> 00:02:39,841
The reason: NEC does not see the boom. An element that goes through a metal boom behaves like a shorter element: in the model it must be shortened.

10
00:02:39,891 --> 00:03:24,991
Calculators, VHF, Boom correction. 30 mm round boom, 4 mm elements, mounted through the boom in contact.

11
00:03:25,041 --> 00:04:07,622
Apply to: all but the radiator, because the radiator is insulated from the boom. Sense: survey → electrical model for the NEC.

12
00:04:07,672 --> 00:04:19,123
The DL6WU / G3SEK formula gives 9.5 mm over the whole element: the table removes the correction from reflector and directors, the radiator stays as it is.

13
00:04:19,173 --> 00:04:25,457
Compare the two calculates both the survey as it is and the electrical model.

14
00:04:25,507 --> 00:04:44,369
Survey: resonance at 145.97 MHz, SWR 7.62 at 144.3, not acceptable. Electrical model: 144.33 MHz, SWR 1.15, 15.7 dBi and F/R 19 dB.

15
00:04:44,419 --> 00:04:55,561
Create the electrical model for the NEC: it opens as a new file, with the correction in the name; the survey file is not touched.

16
00:04:55,611 --> 00:05:14,633
Calculate: 57.3 + j0.7 Ω, SWR 1.15, 15.7 dBi, F/B 19 dB: it is the design antenna again. From here you simulate and optimize on this model; to build, the correction goes back in the sense NEC design → elements to build.

17
00:05:14,683 --> 00:05:31,683
Note: the formula holds up to 0.055 λ of boom; insulated through-boom mounting about half the correction, conductive bracket above the boom about 0.65. Sources: DL6WU, G3SEK, SM5BSZ, DG7YBN.
